      },
      "text": "The human gut harbors approximately 38 trillion microorganisms — roughly equal to the number of human cells — encoding 150× more unique genes than the human genome. These microbes are not parasites but **metabolic partners**: they digest substrates our enzymes cannot, produce essential metabolites, train the immune system, and communicate with the brain. The microbiome is increasingly recognized as a metabolic organ with systemic effects on health and disease."
    },
    {
